#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# kelvin_bei

#% text
# `kelvin_bei(nu, x)` gives the Kelvin function `bei_nu(x)`, the imaginary part of `bessel_j(nu, x*exp(3*pi*im/4))`.

#% text
# `kelvin_bei(x)` gives `bei_0(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The definition is DLMF 10.61.1. `kelvin_bei(0)` is 0.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
kelvin_bei(1.0)
#% output
#= 0.24956604003665978

#% cell
kelvin_bei(0)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
kelvin_bei(2, 3.0)
#% output
#= -0.8910223637797722

#% cell
kelvin_bei(5.0)
#% output
#= 0.11603438155020038

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The definition:

#% cell
kelvin_bei(1.5) - im_part(bessel_j(0, 1.5*exp(3*pi*im/4)))
#% output
#= 0.0

